  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Sunday...\n\nOngoing cold advection is producing breezy north winds and\nadvecting higher concentrations of surface-based smoke. Miles City\nand Baker have seen visibility drop to 5SM, and visibilities are\nlower further north along the high line. Some stations are\nreporting air quality in the \"unhealthy for all\" range so keep\nthis in mind if you are venturing outdoors. HRRR seems to have a \ndecent handle on current smoke trends. Expect thickening smoke \nearly to give way to gradual improvement through this afternoon as\nmixed north winds advect cleaner air behind a secondary cold \nfront which arrives early this morning. As surface smoke\nconditions improve, upper level winds will likely continue to\nadvect lofted smoke from the west.\n\nSatellite imagery shows a Canadian trof dropping into northern ID\nand northwest MT. Mid level frontogenesis/instability produced\nareas of showers and a few weak t-storms in the evening over the \nwest, perhaps even a little more than expected as some stations\nreported measurable rain. As the upper trof swings thru northern \nMT expect another period of isolated to scattered showers across \nour north from late morning thru afternoon. Regarding fire wx, the\ncooler temps and higher humidities will lessen fire weather\nconcerns from the past two days, but there remains a window of\nbreezy north winds and near 20% RH over our far east today.\n\nLook for high temps in the 70s today. A 1020mb surface high \nsettles in tonight bringing a seasonably chilly night with lows in\nthe 40s to lower 50s. In fact, a few daily record lows for August\n4th may be approached. Below normal temperatures continue Tuesday\nand Tuesday night. A weak shortwave moving thru northern WY may\nproduce a few showers/weak t-storms in the southeast near the \nWY/MT border Tuesday night. Heights start to rise Wednesday behind\nthis final wave, and temps should respond accordingly with highs \ninto the 80s.\n\nThe 2nd half of the week will see a transition to flat ridging, \nwarmer temps and dry conditions. Expect temps back to the 90s to\nnear 100F by Friday & Saturday, and possibly Sunday. Ensembles\nshow increasing uncertainty as we get into next weekend and\nbeyond. We should see at least slight cooling by Monday if not\nSunday. Wednesday to Sunday will be dry, with high confidence, as\npwats are expected to linger somewhere near 0.50\". By late Sunday\nor Monday as the next trof approaches we should start seeing the\nmoisture increase again, leading to a return to potential \nthunderstorms beginning late Sunday or Monday. Ensembles show a \nfairly strong signal for above normal pwats moving thru the first \npart of next week. JKL",
